OpenBSD PF reference material for 5BSD PF parity
================================================

Source: https://github.com/openbsd/src.git (OpenBSD CVS mirror)
Revision: facc8d7ec5d1f7a22cd5b7f1e8c6ac4990d91244 (2026-10-10), matching the
  OpenBSD 8.0-current amd64 snapshot used as the reference guest; see
  UPSTREAM.json for the snapshot identity, the PF source-alignment check and
  the git tree object ID of every imported subtree.
Imported: 2026-10-11
License: LICENSE (ISC-style OpenBSD notice); per-file notices retained.

Contents (verbatim, upstream relative paths):
  regress/sbin/pfctl, regress/sys/net/pf_{divert,forward,fragment,opts,
  policy,print,state,table,trans}, regress/sys/net/pflog,
  regress/sys/net/pflow                 primary PF parity suites
  regress/sys/netinet/{frag,pmtu,tcpstate}, regress/sys/netinet6/{frag6,rh0}
                                         IP-stack suites using pf as a tool
                                         (secondary, reported separately)
  regress/sys/netinet/ipsec, regress/sys/net/pair
                                         secondary: pflog of IPsec/enc0 traffic
                                         and pflog on pair(4) (epair on 5BSD)

  regress/sys/net/rdomains, regress/sys/net/rtable, regress/sbin/route,
  regress/sbin/ifconfig                 routing domains (rdomain on FIBs) and
                                         route -T / ifconfig rdomain; rtable is
                                         a userland unit test of OpenBSD's ART
                                         routing table code

Not imported (see UPSTREAM.json "not_imported"): usr.sbin/bgpd pftable
integration tests, usr.sbin/relayd (no PF use), sys/kern/pledge/ioctl
(pledge-specific), and suites that only check pf is enabled (sosplice, gif,
loop, mpath, snmpd).
  share/mk/bsd.regress.mk               OpenBSD regress driver; the upstream
                                         Makefiles include it unchanged
  sbin/pfctl                            OpenBSD pfctl (vendor source for the
                                         PF convergence; wired in by
                                         sbin/pfctl/Makefile when adopted)
  usr.sbin/authpf, usr.sbin/ftp-proxy, usr.sbin/tftp-proxy, sbin/pflogd,
  usr.sbin/tcpdump/print-pf{log,sync}.c  OpenBSD PF userland (vendor source)
  sys/net/pf*.[ch], if_pflog.[ch], if_pflow.[ch], if_pfsync.[ch]
                                         OpenBSD kernel PF (vendor reference
                                         for merges into sys/netpfil/pf)

Not present upstream at this revision: regress for pfsync, queues,
syncookies, authpf, pflogd, ftp-proxy/tftp-proxy.

Local changes: the regress corpus, share/mk and sbin/pfctl are verbatim.
Never edit the regress files to make a test pass; expected outputs change
only through a recorded, user-approved compatibility decision kept in the
glue.  The kernel sources under sys/net carry a small number of edits, each
marked with a "5BSD" comment, where the compatibility layer cannot express
the difference (list: git diff of contrib/openbsd-pf/sys/net against the
import commits; summary below).  Keep them minimal so an update is close
to a re-import.

5BSD edits in usr.sbin (marked "5BSD"):
  authpf/authpf.c  without fdescfs(4) on /dev/fd, /dev/fd/N above 2 does
                 not exist: pass /dev/pf to pfctl as standard input
                 (/dev/fd/0) instead.
  ftp-proxy/ftp-proxy.c, tftp-proxy/tftp-proxy.c  NOPRIV_USER may be set
                 by the build (5BSD uses the existing "proxy" user)

5BSD edits in sys/net (all marked "5BSD"):
  VNET (marked "5BSD: VNET"): global state is defined with PF_VNET_DEFINE*
                 and declared with PF_VNET_DECLARE* (pfvar.h; plain
                 globals outside the kernel), so every vnet runs its own pf;
                 struct tags that share a variable's name are PF_KTAG()ed;
                 static initializers of per-vnet lists move to the glue;
                 pf_norm.c fragment queue/tree are pfn_*; small teardown
                 helpers pfi_vnet_fini(), pf_normalize_vnet_fini(),
                 pf_syncookies_vnet_fini()
  m_tag (marked "5BSD: m_tag"): OpenBSD keeps per-packet pf state in
                 m_pkthdr.pf; 5BSD keeps it in an mbuf tag (PACKET_TAG_PF,
                 sys/sys/mbuf.h) so MSIZE stays 256.  Every access is
                 rewritten mechanically:
                   READ   x->m_pkthdr.pf.F   ->  PF_MTAG_R(x)->F
                   WRITE  x->m_pkthdr.pf.F = ->  PF_MTAG_W(x)->F =
                   and pd.m / pd->m / ctx->pd->m  ->  PF_PDTAG_R/W(pd)
                 (writes are =, |=, &=, ++, --).  Reads never allocate
                 (no tag: default values); writes allocate the tag; the
                 PF_PDTAG forms cache it in pf_pdesc (pfh_m, pfh_t).  After
                 a re-import, apply the same rewrite (see the commit
                 "pf: OpenBSD per-packet state in an mbuf tag").  The
                 single exception: PF_TAG_PROCESSED is set only on packets
                 that already have a tag (nothing reads it on 5BSD).
  jail (marked "5BSD: jail"): the 5BSD extension "jail <name|jid>":
                 struct pf_rule_jail and pf_rule.jail (pfvar.h), lookup.jid
                 (pfvar_priv.h), pf_match_jail() and its rule test (pf.c),
                 rule copy and checksum (pf_ioctl.c); pfctl parse.y grammar,
                 pfctl_parser.c printing, pfctl_optimize.c field table
  pfvar.h        REASON_SET fires the pf::reason:set DTrace probe
  pfvar_priv.h   state-list lock is a sleepable lock (struct sx)
  pf.c           receive interface from m_pkthdr.rcvif (no ph_ifidx);
                 flow id through M_HASHTYPE; DTrace probe hooks;
                 pf_socket_lookup() uses the outbound packet's socket
                 (locked by the 5BSD stack) instead of a pcb table lookup
  pf_if.c        CK_STAILQ interface/group lists; allocate before PF_LOCK
  pf_ioctl.c     p_pid; pfioctl_rw is sleepable; overload table PR_NOWAIT
                 under PF_LOCK; DTrace probe hooks
  pf_norm.c      refragmented packets leave through if_output_obsd()
  pf_lb.c, pf_table.c, pf_syncookies.c   DTrace probe hooks
  if_pflog.[ch], if_pflow.[ch], if_pfsync.c
                 ifnet is if_alloc()ed (sc_if is a macro over sc_ifp);
                 if_output casts; interface counters; no ifq start;
                 sleepable configuration locks; DTrace probe hooks
The compatibility layer and 5BSD attachment live in sys/netpfil/openbsd-pf
(openbsd_compat.[ch], openbsd_net.c, openbsd_ifq.c, pf_freebsd.c,
pf_sdt.c, include/ shadow headers, user/ userland compatibility).

5BSD glue: tests/sys/netpfil/pf/openbsd (Kyua/ATF wrapper; installs this
corpus beneath its srctree/ through tests/srctree.mk) and
tools/test/pf-parity/openbsd-regress (two-guest topology runner: OpenBSD
helper as SRC/RT/ECO, DUT = OpenBSD reference or 5BSD).

OpenBSD-derived PF code: whole files adopted for parity are imported here
with provenance and wired in from their native build directories.  Code
merged into FreeBSD-derived sys/netpfil/pf or sbin/pfctl files is recorded
below (OpenBSD file, revision, local destination) and in the commit message.

Adopted OpenBSD code (pf/parity), all from facc8d7ec5d1:
  sys/net pf*.c, if_pflog, if_pflow, if_pfsync, hfsc, fq_codel, radix (for
  pf tables, renamed obsd_rn_*), sys/netinet in4_cksum.c, inet_nat64.c
                         -> pf.ko (sys/modules/pf)
  sbin/pfctl, etc/pf.os  -> /sbin/pfctl, /etc/pf.os
  share/man/man4 pf.4, pflog.4, pflow.4, pfsync.4, divert.4, share/man/man5
  pf.conf.5, pf.os.5     -> installed by share/man/man4 and man5 (FreeBSD's
                            divert.4 is now ipdivert.4); marked 5BSD
                            additions: pf.conf.5 jail and queueing status, pf.4 VNET,
                            divert.4
  usr.sbin/authpf, ftp-proxy, tftp-proxy -> same programs
  sbin/ifconfig/ifconfig.c pfsync/pflow functions -> sbin/ifconfig/ifpf.c
  sys/sys/mbuf.h struct pkthdr_pf and PF_TAG_* -> 5BSD sys/sys/mbuf.h
  sys/sys/sockio.h SIOC[SG]IFPARENT, SIOC[GS]ETPF{SYNC,LOW} numbers and
  sys/net/if.h struct if_parent -> 5BSD headers

Update procedure: fetch the new pinned commit, replace the subtrees,
regenerate UPSTREAM.json (git rev-parse <commit>:<path>), rerun the reference
baseline on the matching signed OpenBSD snapshot, and record any expected
output changes in the PF parity gap report.

Regression plumbing adjustment (5BSD):
  regress/sys/net/pflow/Makefile escapes Perl $@ as $$@ so bmake does not
  replace it with its automatic target variable. Test expectations unchanged.
